# Field component contract

Apply these rules to public input-like components and shared field primitives.

## DOM and attribute ownership

- `InputWrapper` owns generated IDs and label/description/error relationships. Do not duplicate fallback ID generation in individual field components.
- Consumer `class` and `style` target the **outer field root** so layout styles include label, control, description and error.
- Native form attributes and listeners (`name`, `autocomplete`, `inputmode`, `form`, `data-*`, `aria-*`, `onInput`, `onChange`, etc.) target the real native form control.
- Use `inheritAttrs: false` whenever attrs are deliberately split between wrapper and native control.
- Compose consumer `aria-describedby` with doctui description/error IDs; do not replace either side.

## Native semantics and custom visuals

- Keep a real native `input`, `textarea`, `select` or other semantic control whenever native semantics can represent the behavior.
- Custom Checkbox/Radio/Switch visuals must not replace native keyboard, focus, form, autofill or accessibility behavior. The native input remains the source of checked/disabled/required semantics.
- Visually hidden native inputs must remain focusable when enabled; focus-visible styling must be projected to the visual indicator/track.
- Do not invent `readonly` for boolean controls. Use native `disabled` when interaction must be prevented.

## Size and visual state

- If a component exposes `size`, the size must change meaningful geometry, not only text size. Scale the relevant height, padding, indicator, section, track/thumb or hit-area dimensions through CSS variables/tokens.
- Keep geometry in CSS variables with the `--dui-` prefix. Prefer one shared field-size contract over repeated hard-coded dimensions.
- Error, disabled, readonly, checked/selected and focus-visible states must be visually observable without relying on color alone where practical.
- State styles must remain correct in light/dark themes through semantic tokens.

## Styling API

- `class`/`style` are for the outer field root.
- Use the shared part-based `classNames`/`styles` contract for supported internal parts instead of encouraging consumers to depend on deep DOM selectors.
- Reuse stable part names across the field family (`root`, `label`, `description`, `error`, `control`, `wrapper`, `input`, `section`, `body`, `indicator`, `track`, `thumb`, `labelText`, etc.). Do not create synonymous part names per component without a demonstrated need.
- Public CSS variables and part names are compatibility surface. Document additions and avoid casual renames.

## Tests and documentation

- Start observable field changes with a failing behavior/regression test before implementation when practical.
- Cover generated/custom IDs, label relationships, description + error together, required, disabled, readonly where native, native attrs/listeners, focus behavior and consumer ARIA composition.
- For custom boolean visuals, test that the native input still owns type/name/checked/disabled semantics and keyboard focus.
- Storybook must include a size/state matrix and a realistic multi-component composition for field-family changes.
- VitePress must document root/native attr targeting, accessibility relationships, size behavior, CSS-variable customization and the part-based styles API.
- Keep component metadata synchronized with every public field prop and customization contract.
